e That Killed a Small Business’s Software This happens constantly on a smaller scale: a custom-built tool for inventory management, written by a local developer, gets flagged as a “generic trojan” because it’s unsigned and builds SQL databases. The AV doesn’t “know” it’s your business’s lifeblood; it just sees suspicious behavior. If it’s automatically deleted, the business halts. A non-technical user asking “is it safe?” gets told by an IT security friend “just delete it,” and they lose a $20,000 piece of software they can’t replace. Quarantine lets them call that friend and say, “Hey, I tried to delete it, but it’s in a quarantine box—can you check if it’s really bad?” --- So why does your cousin, who works in IT security, say that? Because **they’re giving you a rule that assumes you cannot tell the difference**, and they’re right. If you, a non-expert, tried to judge every flag yourself, you’d probably talk yourself into running actual malware. So they institute a zero-trust policy: “If it barks, it’s a dog.” And in your environment, that policy has worked perfectly. But that policy is safe for you precisely because the quarantine button is the backup plan for when the cousin themselves, or another IT professional, needs to step in and reverse a mistake. The cousin’s expertise isn’t invalidated by the existence of false positives; rather, their job partly consists of cleaning up the mess when those edge cases happen. **In short: your cousin’s advice is an excellent rule of thumb for a home user, but it’s only safe because the software designers knew it couldn’t be an absolute truth. The quarantine folder is the seatbelt that protects you from the one time the system crashes—even if you’ve never crashed yourself.**
